A||||7||Cheever2||Question about satellite TV||||5||
Z||000000||Cheever2||10-01-2000||01:06 AM||||From what I read, I think I want satellite TV.  My question is this:  can you just get the equipment and install it, and start getting at least some basic stations... or is it absolutely NECESSARY to sign up for monthly payments to get some package programming deal?  I asked Radio Shack but of course they want me to sign up for the package, and wouldn't really answer my question.  And my neighbors who have dishes just assumed they had to get the package and didn't ask, so they don't know.  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||RossT||10-01-2000||01:17 AM||||The sat. signals are all scrambled.  You have to sign up for some kind of package.  They then unscramble the signal for you particular smart card#.<P>I have dishnetwork, and they do have there help channel unscrambled, so when you first set up, you will get something.<P>My boxes have a seamless local/sat link, so I don not have to press an A/B switch when switching between the sat. and local signal.  All I do is either channel up or down and the box will switch the input signal from sat or local input.  Very nice to have if you surf alot.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||10-01-2000||02:48 AM||||i dont own one, but ive heard that you need these special code cards that give you the special access to unscramble the channels.  <P>I seen some of the cards sold on ebay, and you can get unlimited, free, cable until the services figure out that you are getting free cable.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cheever2||10-01-2000||04:21 AM||||So, what should I expect to pay monthly?  What are the good brands and the good programming packages?  I don't want the movie channels like HBO & Cinimax etc... I want SpeedVision and TV-Land.<BR>Anybody got suggestions?<BR>Got something you'd change if you did it again?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000004||RossT||10-01-2000||04:35 AM||||There are really only two companies.<BR>Dishnetwork and directTV.<P>They are both very good.  I have <A HREF="http://www.dishnetwork.com" TARGET=_blank>www.dishnetwork.com</A>  and unfortuneately speedvision is on their upper level. <P>One goodthing about dishenwork is that they own and make everything.  They make the boxes, they sell the programing, and they own the satelites. One stop shopping. They also can download software updates into you box thru the phone lines,  really neat. I do not think that direct TV does that, But direct TV does have the NFL package and Dishnetwork does not.<P>I have had my system for 3 years and had only one minor problem with a box, they exchanged the box and only charge a small fee. Great customer service.<P>Expect to pay 20 bucks for a 40 chanel (bottom package)...  andthe sky is the limit after that. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||10-01-2000||08:45 AM||||We've had an RCA DSS for about 6 months.  We have just about all the channels in the universe (except for the sports channels) and pay about $80/month.  This includes 32 movie channels, 3 MTVs, 2 VH1s (and a partridge in a pear tree).  The only way I could get Speedvision is by getting one of the sports packages.  I think the cheapest package is around $25/month with DirecTV.  You have to buy your own dish/box, but I got mine for free through a special promotion.  The only real problem I see with DSS is the fact that you can't get true local channels unless you live in a big city (like LA or NYC).  Seems like we pay an extra $5/month for "local" programming which is from Indy (I live a couple hours north so it doesn't do me much good--wife wanted it).  According to DirecTV, the entire country should have true local programming by 2004.  A long way off.  The reception is better IMO than cable, but I tend to lose the signal in a hard rain or snow, but only temporarily.  One thing to watch for is location.  You have to have a direct line to the satellite.  I had a small tree branch that was in the way only when it snowed (snow got on the branch and weighed it down a couple of inches).  Got out the trusty hack saw and solved that problem.<P>Sorry.  I got a little long winded.||||reg||1
Z||000006||loafer87gt||10-01-2000||06:06 PM||||I ordered a card burner from Interesting Devices and I now have access to 999 channels with my DirectTV dish. I ordered the card burner and it showed up at my door in a little brown package the next morning. With shipping from the UK, it cost me $115 Canadian. What this does is descramble all the channels so you can watch all the payper view channels and get all the programming without having to pay any monthly fees. I spent $200 for the virgin card, and $125 for the receiver. Works great.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fearless Freep||10-01-2000||09:41 PM||||Loafer....need to be carefull about that stuff!  Mega fines/jail time if they catch you.  Also, DSSC sends out a "worm" signal (for lack of better words) that has the capabilities to fry those kind of cards etc....course they find you that way as well.  If you have to do it, make sure you remove the card from the sat. box when you are not using the tv!||||reg||1
